1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,408
Raidlingo connects two Twitch communities across a language barrier.

2
00:00:03,408 --> 00:00:05,681
This is the live connection desk.

3
00:00:05,681 --> 00:00:11,361
Seat A is connected to the English channel, and seat B to the Spanish
channel.

4
00:00:11,361 --> 00:00:15,905
A separate bot account is authorized to post translations in both chats.

5
00:00:15,905 --> 00:00:22,722
The backend is written in Go, the interface in Preact, and this demo
uses Codex CLI for translation.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:33,964
These are the actual Twitch chats, shown side by side.

7
00:00:33,964 --> 00:00:39,117
First, an English message goes into channel A: Welcome to our bilingual
stream.

8
00:00:39,117 --> 00:00:45,063
Raidlingo receives the chat event, detects its language, and translates
it for the other channel.

9
00:00:45,063 --> 00:00:51,802
The bot reply appears in channel B in Spanish, keeping the original
sender attached to the message.

10
00:00:51,802 --> 00:00:56,162
Viewers can stay in their own channel and follow the conversation.

11
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:09,013
The reverse direction was verified in an earlier live test.

12
00:01:09,013 --> 00:01:14,231
Here is that Spanish message in the live desk: Estoy listo para jugar.

13
00:01:14,231 --> 00:01:17,442
Its English translation is, I'm ready to play.

14
00:01:17,442 --> 00:01:22,258
The original stays visible, and the relay status records a successful
delivery.

15
00:01:22,258 --> 00:01:25,870
The bot posted that English reply in channel A.

16
00:01:25,870 --> 00:01:32,693
Raidlingo also ignores the relay bot's own messages, preventing
translated replies from bouncing between the two chats.

17
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:44,555
The same live conversation is available as an OBS browser overlay.

18
00:01:44,555 --> 00:01:47,453
Here, the selected display language is English.

19
00:01:47,453 --> 00:01:51,593
Spanish messages appear in English with their original text underneath.

20
00:01:51,593 --> 00:01:56,148
Sender names and channel labels preserve the context of each line.

21
00:01:56,148 --> 00:02:03,187
The background is transparent, so streamers can place this panel over
their game, camera, or stream graphics.

22
00:02:03,187 --> 00:02:06,085
It updates as new chat events arrive.

23
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:18,779
Back in the desk, the relay status shows successful deliveries and any
posting errors.

24
00:02:18,779 --> 00:02:22,907
This demo covers live text translation and the OBS overlay.

25
00:02:22,907 --> 00:02:26,210
Voice dubbing is planned for the next phase.

26
00:02:26,210 --> 00:02:31,989
Raidlingo brings two channels into one conversation, while each
community keeps its own language.
